{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763387Z","id":"1a9284c6b70d6ece70e6d3cd5b1dcb796c4cd266cacb2ecc0d976541cd2daf9b","body":{"detail":"Every satellite crate (toolpath-convo, toolpath-git, toolpath-github, toolpath-md, toolpath-dot, toolpath-claude, path-cli, ...) takes `toolpath = { workspace = true }` and their published manifests on crates.io require `toolpath ^0.7.0`. This change moves the workspace entry to 0.8.0 and edits production code in several of them (crates/toolpath-convo/src/derive.rs, crates/toolpath-git/src/lib.rs, crates/toolpath-github/src/lib.rs, crates/toolpath-md/src/source/mod.rs add `parent: None`), yet their versions, CHANGELOG entries and site/_data/crates.json rows are unchanged. scripts/release.sh runs scripts/check-registry-compat.py before publishing; against this branch it fails:\n\n    toolpath-convo 0.11.1: INCOMPATIBLE  toolpath ^0.7.0 (registry) vs 0.8.0 (workspace)\n    toolpath-git 0.6.0: INCOMPATIBLE ...  toolpath-github 0.6.0 ... toolpath-md 0.7.0 ... toolpath-dot 0.5.0 ...\n    registry compatibility check failed for: toolpath-convo, toolpath-git, toolpath-github, toolpath-md, toolpath-dot\n\nThe script's own explanation (scripts/release.sh:173-185) is that publishing toolpath 0.8.0 alone leaves consumers such as pathbase (which pins `toolpath = \"0.7\"` and `toolpath-convo = \"0.11\"`) with two toolpath majors in one graph and E0308 type mismatches. CLAUDE.md's release checklist (\"Versioning and release checklist\") requires a bump whenever a crate's dependencies change.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":27},"end":{"line":27},"content_hash":"cfdd8cf41c8921e6f4831ae1ddcb346e55ba0a21a844a404c7653512fd45bd8a"},"suggested_fix":"Bump every workspace crate that depends on toolpath (patch is enough for the satellites; path-cli per its own rule), add their CHANGELOG lines and update site/_data/crates.json, then re-run `scripts/release.sh` (dry run) until the registry compatibility check passes.","summary":"toolpath is bumped to 0.8.0 but no crate that depends on it is bumped, so scripts/release.sh refuses to publish this workspace","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"FAQ.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763445Z","id":"d75fa1acde010e69d88a900d02220e126e8b20f763e4b5f0691c5c5f5e1ccfdd","body":{"detail":"RFC.md \"Toolpath Base (deprecated)\" (RFC.md:663-678) now says the `toolpath:<path-id>/<step-id>` base form is deprecated and \"Writers MUST use path.parent instead\", and crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:532 marks `Base::toolpath` `#[deprecated]`. FAQ.md:40-42 (\"Can I use Toolpath without a VCS?\") still answers: \"A path's `base` can use a `toolpath:` URI to branch from another path's step, creating a pure Toolpath chain with no VCS backing.\" The FAQ is published at /faq/ (site/pages/faq.md:41 carries the same sentence; site/eleventy.config.js:95 rewrites the RFC's FAQ link to it), so the site contradicts the RFC it links to.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":40},"end":{"line":42},"content_hash":"81037cd0b3434b5331de613cedb60bf29dfdea1cec2ed45283b7b23f9479c1c7"},"suggested_fix":"Rewrite the FAQ answer around `path.parent` (continuation/fork from a step in another document) and `file:///` bases; regenerate site/pages/faq.md if it is derived.","summary":"FAQ still tells writers to use the toolpath: base URI that this change deprecates","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763513Z","id":"89687a0457f96d18da9369221151f1321147764ba88459a06804ca5f4c3ef041","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:432-434: \"the branch merged at step m starts at m.parents[1..] and follows parents[0] until it reaches a step on m's own first-parent chain.\" Read literally, the step on m's chain is reached and so included. The design document this section implements, docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:119-121, states the same rule with \"exclusive\", and its own example (agent-call ... agent-result) only works if the fork step `agent-call` is not part of the subagent branch. Two readers implementing `branches/{step_id}` from the RFC alone can disagree on whether the fork step is in the branch.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":432},"end":{"line":434},"content_hash":"5bb687b3795cc39b2857e05040691a1a9b7791c26868faaddf4d7dc141d429b5"},"suggested_fix":"Add the word: \"... until it reaches a step on m's own first-parent chain, exclusive (the fork step is on the main line, not in the branch)\".","summary":"The branch-slicing rule does not say whether the first-parent-chain step it stops at belongs to the branch","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763554Z","id":"59d1f188cd09d471a09895f20da3a93d3f7c036c79e20cf7a980ae567bd1ace1","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:693 (\"names the step in another, frozen path\") and RFC.md:725 (\"resumed after the parent path froze\") introduce a lifecycle state the format RFC does not define anywhere (`grep -n frozen RFC.md` finds only these two lines). The same wording is in crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:152, crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json:362 and CHANGELOG.md:10. Freezing is a Pathbase server behaviour (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:184-185: \"The server owns freezing\"); a document with `parent` is well-formed whether or not any store has frozen the parent, so a reader of the RFC cannot tell what, if anything, they must check.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":693},"end":{"line":694},"content_hash":"058207162042804526d5587cd472f2f7b45362c5955aee9be9442f45cf1d007a"},"suggested_fix":"Either define the term once (e.g. in Vocabulary: a path a store no longer accepts steps for) and say it is a store-level condition the format does not verify, or drop \"frozen\" from the format text and describe the relation as \"the same work, resumed after the parent path ended\".","summary":"Path Parent requires the parent path to be \"frozen\" but the RFC never defines frozen","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763594Z","id":"8faa873be3ee6adb5c1ad86e646ab96a79c3ffcde1c08a92d8723a46ea6586ae","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:72 defines base as \"The root context (repo, commit) a path branches from\", but RFC.md:628-631 and RFC.md:622 now define it as \"the repository or directory state the work ran against\" and move lineage to `path.parent`. RFC.md:77 defines fork as \"Where paths diverge\", while RFC.md:726 uses `fork` as a `parent.relation` value meaning new work rooted at an older step of another path, and RFC.md:437 calls the tool-use step inside one path the \"fork point\". The table is the first thing a reader meets; the new `parent`/`continuation` rows were added but the two rows they interact with were not revised.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":72},"end":{"line":77},"content_hash":"1d07d4670812f6b1e88925158411b709039ff43772c104aa8d90eb842b724628"},"suggested_fix":"Reword `base` to the \"ran against\" definition and either give `fork` both senses (a divergence point within a path; a `parent.relation` naming new work rooted at an older step of another path) or rename the vocabulary entry to \"fork point\".","summary":"Vocabulary rows for base and fork no longer match the meanings the rest of the RFC gives them","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:22:35.763632Z","id":"344e14f878b29c40c6f747fe73a6a484fa6102a9f55b569a441be5b588f4c909","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:665-666 keeps the old meaning (\"names a step in another path within the same graph\") and RFC.md:676-678 says \"Writers MUST use path.parent instead\". Two gaps: (1) `path.parent.uri` is required and is \"The document holding the parent path\" (RFC.md:717); nothing says what a writer puts there when the parent path is in the same document, so the one case the deprecated form handled has no stated replacement. (2) Nothing says what a reader does with a document that still carries `base.uri: toolpath:...` (treat it as an opaque base with no lineage? synthesise a `parent` with which `relation`?). Existing documents and `Base::toolpath` callers (crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:532) will keep producing it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":663},"end":{"line":678},"content_hash":"db9827e8c415180a53c7003cb508d0cdbb3e32f46bed81bd5d37b0445909d9ac"},"suggested_fix":"Add two sentences: readers MUST treat a `toolpath:` base as an opaque base URI and MUST NOT infer lineage from it; and for a parent in the same document, writers use the document's own URI (or `graph_ref`) in `parent.uri`.","summary":"The deprecated Toolpath Base section neither gives readers a rule for existing toolpath: bases nor a path.parent form for the same-graph case it covered","tags":["review","review:pr-354-r1","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"Cargo.toml","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925223Z","id":"56d5234d918dee93d910a3fa963452af5c4c26e90e81298d514b7c7ab2e0aa61","body":{"detail":"Cargo.toml:27 and crates/toolpath/Cargo.toml:3 move toolpath to 0.8.0; no other crates/*/Cargo.toml version changes in `git diff origin/main...HEAD`. Running the check scripts/release.sh:201 invokes, with every crates/*/Cargo.toml name=version and all satellites in --skip:\n\n    python3 scripts/check-registry-compat.py path-cli=0.29.0 ... toolpath=0.8.0 --skip toolpath-codex toolpath-convo ...\n    toolpath-codex 0.6.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n      toolpath ^0.7.0 (registry) vs 0.8.0 (workspace)\n    toolpath-convo 0.11.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-copilot 0.1.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-cursor 0.2.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-dot 0.5.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-gemini 0.6.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-git 0.6.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-github 0.6.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-md 0.7.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-opencode 0.5.0: INCOMPATIBLE\n    toolpath-pi 0.6.1: INCOMPATIBLE\n    registry compatibility check failed for: toolpath-codex, toolpath-convo, toolpath-copilot, toolpath-cursor, toolpath-dot, toolpath-gemini, toolpath-git, toolpath-github, toolpath-md, toolpath-opencode, toolpath-pi\n    exit=1\n\nThe affected set is wider than the finding lists: toolpath-codex, toolpath-copilot, toolpath-cursor, toolpath-gemini, toolpath-opencode and toolpath-pi fail too. CLAUDE.md:166-174 (\"Versioning and release checklist\") requires crate Cargo.toml, workspace Cargo.toml and site/_data/crates.json to move together on a bump. `concern` is the right kind: nothing fails before a release is attempted, but scripts/release.sh will refuse to publish this workspace as-is.","kind":"comment","references":"1a9284c6b70d6ece70e6d3cd5b1dcb796c4cd266cacb2ecc0d976541cd2daf9b","summary":"confirmed: the registry compatibility check fails for every satellite crate that depends on toolpath","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"FAQ.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925253Z","id":"cda1def350a93147203432ef6b42514a3885e59089cf51b38a6b6a8028e0c8bf","body":{"detail":"FAQ.md:40-42: \"A path's `base` can use a `toolpath:` URI to branch from another path's step, creating a pure Toolpath chain with no VCS backing.\" site/pages/faq.md:41 carries the same sentence. Neither file is in `git diff origin/main...HEAD --stat`. RFC.md:663-678 (\"Toolpath Base (deprecated)\"): \"This form is deprecated. ... Writers MUST use `path.parent` instead.\" crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:530-533 marks `Base::toolpath` `#[deprecated(since = \"0.8.0\", note = \"name the step in `PathIdentity::parent` instead\")]`. site/eleventy.config.js:95 rewrites `(./FAQ.md)` links in the RFC to `(/faq/)`, so the published RFC links to a FAQ that contradicts it. `concern` fits: published documentation now instructs writers to do what the spec forbids.","kind":"comment","references":"d75fa1acde010e69d88a900d02220e126e8b20f763e4b5f0691c5c5f5e1ccfdd","summary":"confirmed: FAQ.md:40-42 and site/pages/faq.md:41 still recommend the toolpath: base URI the RFC now deprecates","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925267Z","id":"7006e74fb003b29ebe8934e70e1a5bf25e7462e7cb648567a949a22f63cb49e5","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:72: `| **base** | The root context (repo, commit) a path branches from|`. RFC.md:622: \"**base**: The repository state the work ran against (repo + ref + branch)\"; RFC.md:628-631: \"anchors the path to the repository or directory state the work ran against ... Lineage between paths is a different fact and lives in `path.parent`.\" RFC.md:77: `| **fork** | Where paths diverge |`; RFC.md:726 defines `relation: fork` as \"New work rooted at an older step of the parent path\"; RFC.md:436-437 calls the tool-use step inside a path \"the fork point\". The new `parent` and `continuation` rows (RFC.md:74-75) are in the diff; rows 72 and 77 are not. `suggestion` is the right kind: a terminology inconsistency, not a defect in the format.","kind":"comment","references":"8faa873be3ee6adb5c1ad86e646ab96a79c3ffcde1c08a92d8723a46ea6586ae","summary":"confirmed: the vocabulary rows for base and fork were not revised to match the new body text","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925277Z","id":"66224b613e2a9f70a99a61a21d17bb4499b4996805beba7d1ef8d20f50e35913","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:432-434: \"the branch merged at step *m* starts at `m.parents[1..]` and follows `parents[0]` until it reaches a step on *m*'s own first-parent chain.\" docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:119-121: \"start at `m.parents[1..]`, walk `parents[0]` until reaching a step on `m`'s own first-parent chain, exclusive.\" The RFC example at RFC.md:436-437 makes the tool-use step the fork point on the main line, which only matches the design document's exclusive reading. `suggestion` fits: one missing word in normative prose, with the intended meaning recoverable from the example.","kind":"comment","references":"89687a0457f96d18da9369221151f1321147764ba88459a06804ca5f4c3ef041","summary":"confirmed: RFC.md:432-434 omits the \"exclusive\" the design document states for the branch slice","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925286Z","id":"115b52188daa9d6a9c12677707c7e419294fa6f8745146215407adf064a5c76c","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:665-666 still defines the form as naming \"a step in another path within the same graph\"; RFC.md:676-678 says only \"This form is deprecated. ... Writers MUST use `path.parent` instead.\" No sentence tells a reader how to treat a document carrying `base.uri: toolpath:...` (`grep -n 'toolpath:' RFC.md` -> lines 196, 665, 671, 761, 785; none is a reader rule). RFC.md:717 defines `parent.uri` as \"The document holding the parent path. Same URI forms as `$ref`, no fragment\" and RFC.md:783-785 lists those forms as URLs, `file:///` and `toolpath://archive-name/path-id`; `grep -n -i 'same document' RFC.md` returns nothing, so the same-graph case has no stated `parent.uri` value. crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:530-540 keeps `Base::toolpath` producing the form. `suggestion` is right: a specification gap, not a failing behaviour.","kind":"comment","references":"344e14f878b29c40c6f747fe73a6a484fa6102a9f55b569a441be5b588f4c909","summary":"confirmed: the deprecated section gives neither a reader rule for existing toolpath: bases nor a parent.uri form for the same-document case","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"RFC.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T16:27:24.925296Z","id":"21145918c65df4cde9ccb836126cf7184471a0f876368c6c91ed1dc5ee5ff74e","body":{"detail":"RFC.md:693: \"`path.parent` names the step in another, frozen path that this path continues from.\" RFC.md:725: \"The same work, resumed after the parent path froze.\" `grep -n -i frozen RFC.md` returns only line 693 (line 725 uses \"froze\"); the Vocabulary table RFC.md:68-77 has no entry for it. The same wording is in crates/toolpath/src/types.rs:150-152, crates/toolpath/schema/toolpath.schema.json:362 and CHANGELOG.md:10. Freezing is defined only as a Pathbase server behaviour in docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md:184-185 (\"The server owns freezing\"). `suggestion` fits: an undefined term in the spec, with nothing for a format reader to verify.","kind":"comment","references":"59d1f188cd09d471a09895f20da3a93d3f7c036c79e20cf7a980ae567bd1ace1","summary":"confirmed: the RFC uses frozen/froze in Path Parent without defining the term anywhere","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
